It rather depends on whether or not you have direct access to the Internet or whether
you have to negotiate firewalls etc.

Assuming you DO have direct access do as follows :

# cd to some directory where you want the file to be placed

# ftp datacom.ee.ubc.ca

When requested for the username enter one of the following :

	1 . A proper registered username for that host
     or 2 . anonymous               <-------------------- this assumes anonymous ftp
							  access is set up on the host

When requested for the password wnter one of the following  :

	1 . A proper registered password for the username if you did not ener anonymous
	    in response to the username request.
     or 2 . Your email address if you used anonymous (ie spowell@sun1.iusb.edu)

All being well you will get some sort of response saying that you are logged in.

At the ftp prompt do as follows

ftp> cd /pub/pc/win3

ftp> get  wqa0595.exe

ftp> quit


You should now be back on your local machine with the file you need.

A note : You may need to set the transfer mode in ftp from ASCII to BINARY (also called
IMAGE)

How to do this varies beteen implementations of ftp but usually typing image at the ftp
prompt does the trick... you will need to do this prior to issuing the get wqa0595.exe


Hope this helps

Jonathan



