> I was wondering if someone could mail me some examples of how to
> setup dns tables (bind4.9) for the following situation:
> 
> A single host (aaa) in the domain ddd.com is already setup
> 
> I like to add the capability to to have this host (aaa) also
> serve a new domain (nnn.com) without adding any new machines
> so that people can send mail to and even access web pages in
> the new domain which is actually served and run by aaa.ddd.com
> 
> 
> Any help, pointers to literature and/or sample would be appreciated
> 
> 
> thanks
> steve

I've seen some examples of this, but I too would love to talk to someone with
BIND 4.9 experience, especially if they're running a DEC alpha.  I'm new
to this particular bill of fare, though I've done DNS before, I never
needed to alias anything except for transitionally when a machine was
going down during a replacement.  I have customers who wish to have their
own domains, say, aardvark.com, but are unwilling or unable to purchase
their own machines.

One solution which occours to me is to have domain.db records for each of
these critters, with a whole soa, NS, etc. entry for each of them.

I suspect that I then need to have an entry within named.boot which
indicates that I am the primary NS for that domain (as if there were new
addresses involved), with the entries pointing to the domain.db file
instead of being a trad. number.in-addr.arpa. and I'm done with DNS. 

I then would need to configure MX records, and sendmail to reflect these
changes, and I'm just not a postmaster by any means, though I have read as
much as I could find in the last few weeks on both DNS and MX, I'm still
in need of that "applied to your specific situation, young man, you need
to insert tab a into slot b, before gluing." 

Right?  Wrong?  I don't know!  :).  I don't really want to broadcast bad
packets and DNS entries even for the hour it takes to see if it works if
someone can fill me in a bit on this question from experience, I would
consider marrying any eligible members of your family who have come to
live with you, or feeding your finches while you vacation, or even saying,
"Damn!  Thanks so much!"


Many thanks in advance

