This one has us baffled, as well as DEC :-<

We're exporting some directories on our AlphaServer 1000 4/200 running
OSF/1 3.2.  Other machines on the network can mount the directories just
fine, but major accessing of the mounted directories causes the nfs
server on the Alpha to hang.

On an AIX box, doing a tar of of the mounted directories works
fine for a bit, but then it starts timeing out.  If the tar process
isn't stopped, nfs on the Alpha will really hose up, to where no system
can access it's exports.  If further left unchecked, the whole system on
the Alpha will effectively lock up -- you can't log in, xterms die, and
the console even locks.  It was left like this for a couple hours
yesterday, before we gave up and halted it.

On a UnixWare box, trying to copy a large file to a mounted directory
on the Alpha causes nfs on the Alpha to hang to where no one can access
it.  I tried killing the 'cp', but it stays in the process list.
The file being copied to the Alpha doesn't show up AT ALL on the
Alpha until (after the system locks up) it is rebooted (and then it is 0
length).

We have a PCI ethernet card using DEC's Tulip interface.  We received a
kernel patch for it, but it didn't help.
We're running 'mountd -n -i', 'nfsd' with 8 threads, and 7 'nfsiod's.

'uerf' contains NO error messages ('uerf' just shows system startup as
the last entry).  And viewing netstat before the system locks shows < 10
errors and only a small percentage of collisions.

Sorry for the long-windedness, but I thought the more info, the better.
We're baffled.  Has anyone else had similar problems?  I saw a post not
long ago about poor performance on an OSF/1 NFS server, but nothing like this.

Any help/comments would be GREATLY appreciated.

TIA,  Tim
