Timothy M. Wieman (timw@isac.hces.com) wrote:
: This one has us baffled, as well as DEC :-<

: We're exporting some directories on our AlphaServer 1000 4/200 running
: OSF/1 3.2.  Other machines on the network can mount the directories just
: fine, but major accessing of the mounted directories causes the nfs
: server on the Alpha to hang.

After we upgraded our NFS server (DEC 3000/900 AXP) from V3.0 to V3.2A we 
found that requests on all of our NFS clients hang every now and then.  
We have to reboot our server about every three days.  It seems that 
when the NFS load becomes high enough the nfsd daemon on the server hangs
and this stops all the NFS requests to be served. Otherwise the server 
works ok. Furthermore, killing nfsd is impossible, the machine does not 
shut down because nfsd does not die and hard reset is the only thing we 
can do for the server.  After the reboot the server works again ok and 
all of NFS clients continue if they have waited for the disk of the server 
but the server hangs again later.

I reported this earlier in this newsgroup and got a few suggestions
that Suns or IBM's using NFS V2 protocol could cause the problem.
I removed all non-OSF/1 NFS clients to test this but the NFS server
still hangs.  I reported this also to DEC but all they can say is that
they do not have a patch for this!

The fastest way to get your NFS server to work is to downgrade to V3.0 
(if your applications let you do this) where the NFS server daemon (nfsd) 
implementation is different from V3.2. 

BTW, we have upgraded OSF/1 V1.2->V1.3->V2.1->V3.0->V3.2A and all 
upgrades except V1.2->V1.3 have caused us a lot of trouble: some
of the applications stop working, machice crashes and panics, weak 
performance, hangs etc.  In my opinion V1.3 has been the most stable 
version, after that DEC has been too busy to include new features
into their operating system that they have forgot what the 
reliability means for their customers.

